From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 17:13:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10804/11362] Fix my own planner-prompt regression: the design
 document stopped being written

The document research-roadmap-vNEXT.md was rewritten on every planning run for
.595, .596, .598, .599, .600, .601, .602 twice and .603 -- eight consecutive. Milestone
.604, planned at 18:05Z with 7 tasks, did not rewrite it; the file still reads V603
with 13 tasks, exp6885 through exp6897. .604 is the first planning run after my
prompt change 1fe5e74d83 loaded at the 15:06Z restart. Eight-for-eight before,
zero-for-one after.

The evidence is n=1 after the change and the entry says so. An unbroken eight-run
prior can break for an unrelated reason. But the suspect instruction is mine, the
harm is concrete, and waiting for .605 to confirm costs a milestone.

The plausible mechanism is the ordering sentence I added: "Write FILE 2 first, then
make FILE 1's task contract describe exactly what FILE 2 contains." That can read as
permission to treat FILE 1 as a follow-up rather than a required output, and a
planner out of turns after FILE 2 ships without it.

The result is worse than the defect I was fixing. The original divergence was a
document over-claiming tasks within the same milestone; a document left from the
previous milestone guarantees a mismatch, and .602 showed a failed root contract
cascade-blocks its dependents, 3 of 4 there.

This replaces the ordering sentence with an explicit both-files-required rule naming
the .604 incident as its reason, and leaves the consistency requirement unchanged. 6
tests still green. Takes effect on the next conductor restart.

What settles it: .605's planning run. Document rewritten with a matching contract
closes both the defect and this regression; skipped again means my instruction is not
the cause and the planner's file-writing step needs direct attention.

